<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Illinois</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Illinois is a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwestern United States</a>, lying between Lake Michigan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> river, with Springfield as its capital and Chicago as its largest city. Named after the Illinois Confederation, it was part of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois-country">Illinois Country</a> before passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1763 and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which admitted it as a state in 1818. In the 1840s Nauvoo, Illinois, was the centre of the Latter-day Saints.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/illinois.jpg" alt="Illinois" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Illinois.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Illinois lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a> and is one of the eight states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region. Most of its western border with Missouri and Iowa is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wabash-river">Wabash River</a> forms part of the eastern border with Indiana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> marks the southern border with Kentucky, and the northeast reaches into Lake Michigan. The state lies entirely in the Interior Plains, with a small unglaciated area, the Driftless Area, in the extreme north-west, where Charles Mound, at 376 metres, is the highest natural point. The Illinois River bisects the state from north-east to south-west.</p><p>The name is the modern spelling of what early French missionaries and explorers called the Illinois Native Americans. It derives from the Miami-Illinois verb irenwe·wa, 'he speaks the regular way', which passed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> and then French. The older explanation that it means 'men' is not supported by the Illinois language, and the spelling Illinois began to appear in the early 1670s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>American Indians of successive cultures lived along the waterways for thousands of years; the Koster Site shows 7,000 years of continuous habitation. Cahokia, the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> and urban centre of the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a>, stood near present-day Collinsville. It had more than 100 platform and burial mounds, a large plaza and a woodhenge of sacred cedar, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monks-mound">Monks Mound</a>, 30 metres high, is the largest pre-Columbian structure north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>.</p><p>The French were the first Europeans to arrive, settling near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and Illinois rivers in the 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois-country">Illinois Country</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. After American independence in 1783 made the Mississippi the national boundary, settlers arrived from Kentucky by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>. Illinois became part of the Northwest Territory and achieved statehood in 1818. John Deere's self-scouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> plough turned the prairie into productive farmland. In the mid-19th century the Illinois and Michigan Canal and a large railroad network made the state a transportation hub, and Chicago became the world's fastest-growing city by the late 19th century. By 1900 industrial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining jobs had attracted immigrants from Eastern and Southern Europe, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-migration-african-american">Great Migration</a> from the South established a large black community.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Springfield is the capital and Chicago the largest city, and the Chicago metropolitan area holds about three-quarters of the state's 12.8 million residents. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> lie in Illinois: the Cahokia Mounds and part of the Wright architecture site. Illinois honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> with its slogan Land of Lincoln, and the Abraham Lincoln Presidential Library and Museum is in Springfield.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Illinois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maize, worked with hoes of Mill Creek chert quarried in southern <b>Illinois</b>, became a staple alongside native seed crops such as goosefoot and sumpweed, squash, and the fish, waterfowl and deer of the river bottoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Another holds that it began in western <b>Illinois</b> and spread by diffusion to southern Ohio, while the Havana Hopewell tradition spread up the Illinois River into south-western Michigan, giving rise to Goodall Hopewell.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on maize, grown with squash, sunflower and native seed plants in the fertile soils of river floodplains and worked with hoes bladed with stone or shell; the chert of Mill Creek in southern <b>Illinois</b> was quarried for hoe blades traded across the Midwest.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1855 Mary Meachum, a free Black woman, tried to help eight or nine people cross the Mississippi to <b>Illinois</b>; word reached police agents and slave catchers who waited on the far shore, and the attempt failed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In June 1910 Todd, a state factory inspector, had joined an automobile campaign across <b>Illinois</b> for women's right to vote, speaking for working women on wages, conditions and hours.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bread-and-roses">Bread and Roses</a></li><li><blockquote>Abraham Lincoln and Senator Stephen Douglas met in Ottawa, <b>Illinois</b>, for the first of seven debates on the spread of slavery.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
